{"version": 2, "width": 92, "height": 34, "timestamp": 1787038760, "idle_time_limit": 2.0, "env": {"SHELL": "/bin/zsh", "TERM": "xterm-256color"}}
[0.505007, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m  Medical-Device Regulatory Affairs KG\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  FDA device data as a graph — clearances, product codes, 21 CFR\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[0.577034, "o", "  \u001b[1m28,496\u001b[0m nodes   \u001b[1m25,310\u001b[0m edges\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  [enter]\u001b[0m"]
[3.68496, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  1. What is in this graph, and where did it come from?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Before any answer is worth trusting. Every node carries the FDA endpoint\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  it came from, how it was obtained and when — so the graph answers this\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  itself rather than asking you to take a caption on trust.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (n) WHERE n.source IS NOT NULL\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN n.source AS fda_endpoint, n.extraction_method AS method,\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[3.685016, "o", "  \u001b[36mn.retrieved_at AS retrieved, count(n) AS records\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY records DESC\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[3.727401, "o", "  \u001b[1mfda_endpoint           method  retrieved                  records\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m---------------------  ------  -------------------------  -------\u001b[0m\r\n  device/510k            api     2026-08-12T05:37:59+00:00  19127  \r\n"]
[3.727541, "o", "  device/classification  api     2026-08-12T05:37:59+00:00  9369   \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m42 ms\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  [enter]\u001b[0m"]
[7.193898, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  2. 21 CFR 870.5150 is amended. Who is affected?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  The question this graph exists for. Today it is someone reading a rule\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  and going through a portfolio by hand.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (r:Regulation)<-[:GOVERNED_BY]-(:ProductCode)<-[:CLASSIFIED_AS]-(s:Submission)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE r.cfr_section = '870.5150'\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN s.applicant AS applicant, count(s) AS clearances\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY clearances DESC LIMIT 6\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[7.206499, "o", "  \u001b[1mapplicant                 clearances\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m------------------------  ----------\u001b[0m\r\n  Penumbra, Inc.            27        \r\n  Inari Medical             24        \r\n  Inari Medical, Inc.       23        \r\n  Possis Medical, Inc.      20        \r\n  Ekos Corp.                17        \r\n  Vascular Solutions, Inc.  14        \r\n"]
[7.206579, "o", "\r\n  \u001b[32m12 ms\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  [enter]\u001b[0m"]
[10.704912, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  3. How many clearances in total sit under that one rule?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  The FDA stamps the regulation number onto every clearance, so this is an\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  exact count rather than a name match. It agrees with what the API returns\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  for the same filter, arrived at independently.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (r:Regulation)<-[:GOVERNED_BY]-(:ProductCode)<-[:CLASSIFIED_AS]-(s:Submission)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE r.cfr_section = '870.5150'\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN count(s) AS clearances_affected\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[10.716263, "o", "  \u001b[1mclearances_affected\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-------------------\u001b[0m\r\n  415                \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m11 ms\u001b[0m"]
[10.71639, "o", "\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  [enter]\u001b[0m"]
[14.21364, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  4. Which cardiovascular rules carry the most clearances?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Where the regulatory weight actually sits — useful for deciding which\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  rule changes to watch. The part-870 filter is explicit rather than\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  relying on the load happening to be bounded to cardiovascular.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (r:Regulation)<-[:GOVERNED_BY]-(:ProductCode)<-[:CLASSIFIED_AS]-(s:Submission)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE r.part = '870'\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN r.cfr_section AS cfr_section, count(s) AS clearances\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY clearances DESC LIMIT 6\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[14.239389, "o", "  \u001b[1mcfr_section  clearances\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----------  ----------\u001b[0m\r\n  870.1250     1685      \r\n  870.1130     1219      \r\n  870.1025     894       \r\n  870.2700     832       \r\n"]
[14.239539, "o", "  870.1330     807       \r\n  870.2300     771       \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m26 ms\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  [enter]\u001b[0m"]
[17.722947, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  5. Take one device — what governs it?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  K233820, the Fogarty Arterial Embolectomy Catheter. The device whose\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  summary PDF names a pre-1976 predecessor with no clearance record at all.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (s:Submission)-[:CLASSIFIED_AS]->(p:ProductCode)-[:GOVERNED_BY]->(r:Regulation)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE s.id = 'K233820'\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN s.device_name AS device, s.applicant AS applicant,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mp.product_code AS code, r.cfr_section AS governed_by\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[17.816292, "o", "  \u001b[1mdevice                                                 applicant             code  governed_by\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----------------------------------------------------  --------------------  ----  -----------\u001b[0m\r\n  Fogarty Arterial Embolectomy Catheter with Gate Valve  Edwards Lifesciences  DXE   870.5150   \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m93 ms\u001b[0m"]
[17.816429, "o", "\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  [enter]\u001b[0m"]
[21.233711, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  6. Which device categories are Class III — the high-risk route?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Class III needs full PMA approval rather than a 510(k). Knowing which\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  categories those are shapes how a portfolio is managed.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (p:ProductCode)-[:GOVERNED_BY]->(r:Regulation)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE p.device_class = '3'\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN r.cfr_section AS cfr_section, p.product_code AS code,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mp.device_name AS category\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY cfr_section LIMIT 6\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[21.247699, "o", "  \u001b[1mcfr_section  code  category                                       \u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----------  ----  -----------------------------------------------\u001b[0m\r\n  876.3350     FHW   Device, Impotence, Mechanical/Hydraulic        \r\n  876.3350     JCW   Prosthesis, Penis, Inflatable                  \r\n  878.4010     PJK   Tissue Adhesive For Internal Use               \r\n  878.4010     PLJ   Tissue Adhesive Internal Use                   \r\n  876.5980     PTK   Pediatric Esophageal Atresia Anastomosis Device\r\n  886.4275     LZP   Aid, Surgical, Viscoelastic                    \r\n"]
[21.247905, "o", "\r\n  \u001b[32m14 ms\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  [enter]\u001b[0m"]
[24.74308, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m  What this is not, yet\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Predicate chains — the fifty-year citation network — are designed but not\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  loaded. The predecessor device is not in the FDA's API; it exists only\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  inside the 510(k) Summary PDF, as free text. That extraction is next, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  how often a name resolves to a real clearance is still unmeasured.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
